  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「メキシカンライム」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%A1%E3%82%AD%E3%82%B7%E3%82%AB%E3%83%B3%E3%83%A9%E3%82%A4%E3%83%A0",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 107250141,
      "text": "メキシカンライム は柑橘類の一種。熱帯アジア原産の低木で、ビアソングと枸櫞の交配種。果実は食用。別名としてキーライム 、マナオが知られている。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Key lime」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Key_lime",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1378339675,
      "text": "The Key lime, also known as West Indian lime, Mexican lime, or Egyptian lime is a type of lime. While it is treated as a species in botanical classification, it originated as a natural hybrid between Citrus micrantha and Citrus medica (citron)."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Key lime」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Key_lime#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1378339675,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "■ History\nIn California in the late 19th century, \"Mexican\" limes were more highly valued than lemons; however, in Florida, they were generally considered weeds. Then, in 1894–95, the Great Freeze destroyed the Florida lemon groves, and farmers replanted Mexican limes instead; they soon became known as the Florida Key Lime, a \"beloved regional crop\". But when the 1926 Miami hurricane ripped them up, they were replanted with the hardier, thornless Persian limes.\nSince the North American Free Trade Agreement came into effect, most Key limes on the United States market have been grown in Mexico, Central America and South America. They are also grown in Texas, Florida, and California.\n\n■ Propagation\n\nThere are various approaches to the cultivation of Key limes. This variety of citrus can be propagated from seed and will grow true to the parent. The seeds must be kept moist until they can be planted, as they will not germinate if allowed to dry out.If the plants are propagated from seed, the seeds should be stored at least 5–6 months before planting. Alternatively, vegetative propagation from cuttings or by air layering may permit fruit production within one year, and from genetically more predictable lines of plants. Another method, digging around a mature tree to sever roots, will encourage new sprouts that can be transplanted to another location.  Clones are often bud grafted into rough lemon or bitter orange to obtain strong root stocks.\nIt is often advisable to graft the plants onto rootstocks with low susceptibility to gummosis because seedlings generally are highly vulnerable to the disease. Useful rootstocks include wild grapefruit, cleopatra mandarin and tahiti limes. C. macrophylla is also sometimes used as a rootstock in Florida to add vigor.",
        "truncated": true
      }
    }
  },
